questions.answer:
CAPL programs consist of: (1) Variable declarations - global variables, message definitions, (2) Event handlers - on message, on signal, on timer, (3) Functions - custom functions for reusable logic, (4) Initialization - code in 'on preStart' and 'on start' blocks.

```c
variables {
  dword gCounter = 0;
  int gErrorCount = 0;
  float gTemperature = 25.0;
  message 0x123 gTestMsg;
}

on preStart {
  // Initialization before simulation starts
  gCounter = 0;
}

on start {
  // Initialization when simulation starts
  write("Simulation started!");
}
```

Use 'on message' handler to capture frames with specific CAN ID. Access frame data using this.data[] array for raw bytes. Use DLC field to verify message length.

```c
on message 0x100 {
  write("Message 0x100 received!");
  write("DLC: %d bytes", this.dlc);

  // Access first 4 bytes as 32-bit value
  dword id_data = this.data[0] | 
                 (this.data[1] << 8) | 
                 (this.data[2] << 16) | 
                 (this.data[3] << 24);

  write("Data: 0x%08X", id_data);
}
```

After loading DBC file, use 'on signal' handler to capture decoded signal changes. Access signal value directly via 'this' variable. Signals automatically handle byte order and scaling.

```c
on signal EngineRPM {
  write("RPM changed to: %d", this);

  if (this > 7000) {
    write("Warning: RPM too high!");
  }
}

on signal VehicleSpeed {
  dword speed_kmh = this;
  write("Speed: %d km/h", speed_kmh);
}
```

Create message object, populate data bytes, then transmit using output() function. Byte order and scaling handled automatically if using signal assignment.

```c
message 0x200 myMsg;

on start {
  myMsg.dlc = 8;
  myMsg.data[0] = 0x01;
  myMsg.data[1] = 0x02;
  myMsg.data[2] = 0x03;
  myMsg.data[3] = 0x04;
  myMsg.data[4] = 0x05;
  myMsg.data[5] = 0x06;
  myMsg.data[6] = 0x07;
  myMsg.data[7] = 0x08;

  output(myMsg);
  write("Message transmitted");
}
```

